 <FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" ><B></b></font>
<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >Well, despite some of the rifraff on the message boards (there, not here), I still think <a href="http://www.artifact-entertainment.com/horizons/" target=_blank>Horizons</a> looks to be one of the most promising upcoming games.  However, its 2 (or more) year development time that it still has ahead of it means that it's quite possible to try out several of the others, and still have room for it later down the road.<br>
<br>
Features of Horizons that I like:<br>
<br>
<ul type=square><li>No Classes.  The "pick a strict mold for your character to fall into and never have any flexibility" model went out with AD&D 2nd edition.  <br>
<br>
<li>Aging.  This will help to keep the powergamers from getting too out of hand.  Mainly those who play a single character in shifts.  It also adds a bit of realism.<br>
<br>
<li>Skill trees.  EQ's one-dimensional "skill tree" is a joke.  You get to a certain level, you go train the skills for that level.  Duh.  This is another feature that adds to the creative control you have over your character.<br>
<br>
<li>Full PVP with consequences.  PKs will (hopefully) find it very had to just randomly PK anyone they want to.  PvP will be open, but if you kill the wrong people you will have to be prepared to deal with any consequences. <br>
</ul><br>
<br>
There are some other cool things, but I think these are some of the basic underlying decisions about the design of the game that will make it work well.  The unique races that they have and the system of blood wars and race wars is also interesting.  Overall, if they accomplish the above listed things and perhaps 75% of the rest, I think it'll be a great game to play.<br>
<br>
__________________<br>
I hate to advocate weird chemicals, alcohol, violence or insanity to anyone...but they've always worked for me.</font></p>

